menu "sys_session"

    config SYS_SESSION_MAX
        int "Sessions"
        default 4
        range 1 16
        help
          Providers attached to a peer: a parent, children, the root's clients.

    #@limit @alias Calls to other boards
    config SYS_SESSION_CALLS
        int "Calls in flight"
        default 32
        range 1 64
        help
          The board's own requests to other boards waiting for an answer
          (sys_session_call); a call to this board takes none. Every call to
          another board is answered, so each write of a remote device in
          flight takes one.

    config SYS_SESSION_PEER_IN_FLIGHT
        int "Calls in flight to one board"
        default 4
        range 1 16
        help
          Calls to one board on the way at once; one more is ERR_SESSION_BUSY
          (no failure: the caller sends it later). Keeps a fast caller from
          queuing a backlog on a slow link.

    #@limit @alias Call timeout @unit ms
    config SYS_SESSION_CALL_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Call timeout (ms)"
        default 1000
        range 50 10000
        help
          A call without an answer this long ends with ERR_SESSION_TIMEOUT:
          over a radio (BLE, MQTT, ESP-NOW) or further than a neighbour.
          Longer than a radio's tail, so a slow answer is not taken for a
          lost one.

    #@limit @alias Wired call timeout @unit ms
    config SYS_SESSION_CALL_TIMEOUT_WIRED_MS
        int "Wired call timeout (ms)"
        default 50
        range 10 1000
        help
          The same to the peer of a session on a WIRED provider (a cable):
          its round trip is about a millisecond.

    config SYS_SESSION_GONE_LISTENERS
        int "Gone-peer listeners"
        default 5
        range 1 8
        help
          sys_session_on_gone and sys_session_on_reached slots, each: the
          connectors, the watches, the dead-man.

    config SYS_SESSION_STREAM_LISTENERS
        int "Stream listeners"
        default 2
        range 1 4
        help
          sys_session_on_stream slots.

    config SYS_SESSION_TIME_PERIOD_S
        int "Time broadcast period (s)"
        default 60
        range 5 3600
        help
          The root sends its time to every board this often (and when a
          client sets it), so the clocks of all boards are the root's.

    #@limit @alias Session timeout @unit ms
    config SYS_SESSION_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Session timeout (ms)"
        default 3000
        range 150 60000
        help
          A child's session with nothing received for this long ends: its
          calls fail, its peers are gone, the hello starts again. The hello
          tells the peer, which sends at least every third of it. The parent's
          and the clients' sessions take the dead-man's time instead
          (sys_session_set_timeout).

endmenu
